===persian===

یکی از کبوترانم برنمی‌گردد،
شعری تازه می‌گویم...
یکی از کبوترانم می‌میرد،
از اندیشه‌ای... سرد... ساکت...
و... بی‌کلام... عبور می‌کنم...
یکی از کبوتران بیمارم را
تنها در قفس... در خونش غلطان می‌بینم...
چنان به خشم می‌آیم... که....
کلاغان بر بام نشسته را
ضحّاک می‌پندارم.
من کی‌ام؟...
در مرگِ پرنده‌ام گریه می‌کنم...
از دوری کبوترانم غمناک می‌شوم...
گاه ساعت‌ها...
در کنار دوست شکارچی‌ام
برای شکار آهویی در دشت پرسه می‌زنم...
و خاطره‌ای خوش از شبی دارم...
که گوسفندی را به تمامی...
پوست کندیم...
بر آتش گرداندیم... و... خوردیم...
آنگاه... به شعری لطیف در رثای...
یک قناری زرد گوش دادیم...
برخیزم...
برخیزم همهٔ کبوترانم را
رها کنم...
نداشتن... تنها راه... از دست ندادن است
۱۳۶۹/۱/۱۷

===machine===

One of my doves does not come back,
I make a new poem...
One of my doves dies,
and I pass through a thought... cold... silent...
and... wordless...
One of my sick doves
alone in its cage... I see rolling in its own blood...
Such a rage comes over me... that....
I take the crows perched on the roof
for Zahhak.
Who am I?...
I weep at the death of my bird...
I turn sorrowful at the absence of my doves...
Sometimes for hours...
beside my hunter friend
I roam the plain to hunt a deer...
and I have a fond memory of a night...
when we skinned a sheep...
whole...
turned it over the fire... and... ate it...
And then... we listened to a delicate poem
in elegy for...
a yellow canary...
Let me rise...
Let me rise and set all my doves
free...
Not having... is the only way... of not losing.
1369/1/17

===lantern===

One of my doves does not come back,
and I make a new poem...
One of my doves dies,
and I pass through a thought... cold... silent...
and... wordless...
One of my sick doves,
alone in its cage... I find rolling in its own blood...
Such a rage comes over me... that....
I take the crows perched on the roof
for Zahhak.
Who am I?...
I weep at my bird's death...
I go sorrowful when my doves are far off...
And sometimes, for hours,
beside my hunter friend
I roam the plain after a deer...
and I keep a fond memory of a night
when we skinned a sheep,
the whole of it,
turned it over the fire... and... ate it...
And then... we listened to a delicate poem
in elegy for
a yellow canary...

Let me rise and set all my doves
free...
Not having... is the only way... of not losing.

1369/1/17

===translation===

===footnotes===

ضحّاک — Zahhak, the tyrant of the *Shahnameh* with serpents growing from his
shoulders, who was fed on human brains. To take the crows on the roof for Zahhak is to see, in
ordinary black birds over a dove's cage, the whole national figure of appetite and cruelty.

The poem's question — من کی‌ام؟, *who am I?* — is asked in earnest and not answered. He weeps over
a dove and hunts a deer; he remembers a night of skinning and eating a sheep whole with pleasure,
and then listening, that same night, to an elegy for a canary. Nothing is reconciled. What arrives
instead is the closing line: نداشتن... تنها راه... از دست ندادن است — *not having is the only way
of not losing* — which is the answer released-doves (page 86) was already reaching for when it let
the doves go hoping they would not come back.

